1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,867
On a phone screen, a new edit has a fraction of a second to justify itself.

2
00:00:06,867 --> 00:00:14,967
YouTube even measures it: for every Short, creators can see how many people viewed it, and how many swiped away.

3
00:00:14,967 --> 00:00:19,967
Inside that tiny window, a quiet revolution has taken place.

4
00:00:19,967 --> 00:00:25,900
A new generation of editors has captured much of the editing community's attention.

5
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:32,767
Many established editors, people who helped build this culture, have found that attention harder to hold.

6
00:00:32,767 --> 00:00:38,067
The easy explanation is that the new editors are simply better.

7
00:00:38,067 --> 00:00:42,767
This documentary tests that explanation instead of assuming it.

8
00:00:42,767 --> 00:00:49,000
Did editing evolve so far that the old standard of excellence stopped being enough?

9
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:54,300
Or did the stage itself change underneath everyone?

10
00:01:04,833 --> 00:01:11,667
The world of fan edits is enormous, and almost all of it is made by individuals.

11
00:01:11,667 --> 00:01:18,533
Football highlights, film characters, anime, nostalgia: cut to music, packed with effects, built to be rewatched.

12
00:01:18,533 --> 00:01:26,333
This film looks at six channels suggested by the VINIXEDZ community: Heisty, Fictic, Eagle Stan, 10Mov, BroxEditZ and Slicx7.

13
00:01:26,333 --> 00:01:33,500
They are not one movement. They edit different subjects, use different tools, and grew at different speeds.

14
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:39,733
Before we start, there is one rule for everything you are about to see.

15
00:01:39,733 --> 00:01:45,033
Every claim on screen is labelled: verified, observed, hypothesis, or speculation.

16
00:01:45,033 --> 00:01:53,767
Verified means we can point to a source. Observed means you can see it in the work, but nobody has measured it.

17
00:01:53,767 --> 00:01:59,700
Hypothesis means a reasoned explanation we have not proven. Speculation means exactly that.

18
00:02:04,167 --> 00:02:08,367
Fan editing is older than YouTube.

19
00:02:08,367 --> 00:02:16,133
Anime music videos, or AMVs, were already circulating in fan communities in the 1990s, cut together on home computers.

20
00:02:16,133 --> 00:02:22,367
By 2009, the hub site AnimeMusicVideos.org listed well over a hundred thousand of them.

21
00:02:22,367 --> 00:02:29,533
The most important tool in this story is older still: After Effects first shipped in January 1993.

22
00:02:29,533 --> 00:02:37,333
It was built by a small company called CoSA, which Aldus bought that same year. Adobe followed in 1994.

23
00:02:37,333 --> 00:02:43,567
For a long time, compositing at that level was associated mainly with professional studios.

24
00:02:43,567 --> 00:02:51,367
Then the plugins arrived. In July 2012, Video Copilot released Element 3D, bringing real-time 3D objects into After Effects.

25
00:02:51,367 --> 00:02:57,933
And tutorials turned techniques that once took years to discover into lessons anyone could follow.

26
00:02:57,933 --> 00:03:05,400
This was the widescreen era: horizontal video, longer montages, and audiences who found edits through search and subscriptions.

27
00:03:09,833 --> 00:03:14,033
Then the screen stood upright.

28
00:03:14,033 --> 00:03:19,333
Short vertical video exploded, and serious editing moved onto the phone.

29
00:03:19,333 --> 00:03:26,200
Alight Motion reached the iPhone App Store in December 2019, bringing keyframes and layers to mobile.

30
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:31,200
In 2020, ByteDance released internationally the editor that became CapCut.

31
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:38,367
On the 14th of September 2020, YouTube announced Shorts, launching it first as a beta in India.

32
00:03:38,367 --> 00:03:43,667
A new grammar spread through this vertical world: the velocity edit.

33
00:03:43,667 --> 00:03:49,900
Speed ramps that slow a moment down, then snap it forward on the beat.

34
00:03:49,900 --> 00:03:54,900
Shakes, flashes, zoom transitions, and tight synchronisation with the music.

35
00:03:54,900 --> 00:04:02,400
The editors who mastered that grammar did something important: they made it the shared language of the community.

36
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:07,400
Everything that came next was built on top of it.

37
00:04:11,833 --> 00:04:16,500
So let's look at how that grammar actually works.

38
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:23,667
A velocity edit is a time remap: the clip's speed changes inside the shot, not between shots.

39
00:04:23,667 --> 00:04:31,167
On a speed graph, the curve drops into slow motion, holds, then accelerates sharply into the next beat.

40
00:04:31,167 --> 00:04:38,933
The shake is a separate layer of motion: fast, decaying offsets in position and rotation, often with motion blur.

41
00:04:38,933 --> 00:04:46,733
A flash or zoom transition hides the cut at its fastest frame, so the eye reads one continuous movement.

42
00:04:46,733 --> 00:04:53,300
All of it is locked to the audio. The beat decides where the energy lands.

43
00:04:53,300 --> 00:05:00,767
Done well, this is real craft. A ramp that feels right is the result of many small decisions.

44
00:05:00,767 --> 00:05:07,000
But notice what it mostly transforms: time and motion, rather than the image itself.

45
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:11,200
That distinction matters for what happened next.

46
00:05:15,633 --> 00:05:21,867
Before looking at the new editors, look at the platform they grew up on.

47
00:05:21,867 --> 00:05:29,633
On the 1st of February 2023, YouTube began sharing advertising revenue from Shorts with creators in its Partner Program.

48
00:05:29,633 --> 00:05:36,800
Creators keep 45 percent of the revenue allocated to them, based on their share of Shorts views.

49
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:42,733
Shorts analytics also show the metric we started with: viewed versus swiped away.

50
00:05:42,733 --> 00:05:49,900
On the 15th of October 2024, Shorts were extended to three minutes for square and vertical uploads.

51
00:05:49,900 --> 00:05:55,533
In March 2024, YouTube said Shorts were averaging 70 billion daily views.

52
00:05:55,533 --> 00:06:01,767
By June 2025, YouTube's CEO Neal Mohan said the figure was over 200 billion.

53
00:06:01,767 --> 00:06:06,467
These are the company's own numbers, not independent audits.

54
00:06:06,467 --> 00:06:12,400
But the direction is clear: an enormous, paid, swipe-driven stage for short edits.

55
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:19,567
A stage like this may reward something specific: the first second, and a reason to watch again.

56
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:28,200
Now, the six channels.

57
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:35,967
BroxEditZ calls itself 'That One Football Editor', and says it edits with After Effects and Topaz's AI video enhancer.

58
00:06:35,967 --> 00:06:43,767
According to vidIQ, the channel joined YouTube in June 2022 and had about 2.78 million subscribers by April 2025.

59
00:06:43,767 --> 00:06:51,567
Slicx7 is also a football channel. Its description says everything is edited in After Effects and Topaz Video Enhancer.

60
00:06:51,567 --> 00:06:58,100
vidIQ lists it as created in 2016, with about 1.52 million subscribers in February 2025.

61
00:06:58,100 --> 00:07:06,200
10Mov focuses on football too, especially Messi and Neymar, and lists After Effects and Topaz Video AI as its tools.

62
00:07:06,200 --> 00:07:12,767
Heisty edits movies, shows, anime and nostalgia, and lists After Effects, Topaz and Alight Motion.

63
00:07:12,767 --> 00:07:19,300
Eagle Stan builds fandom montages around film and TV characters, often set to phonk-style music.

64
00:07:19,300 --> 00:07:28,033
Fictic is harder to pin down: several different channels use that name, so any numbers must be tied to one exact channel.

65
00:07:28,033 --> 00:07:34,267
Three of the six are football editors. That pattern is worth keeping in mind.

66
00:07:34,267 --> 00:07:41,133
And four of them name the same tool besides After Effects: Topaz, an AI video upscaler.

67
00:07:41,133 --> 00:07:48,300
That suggests a shared workflow: source clips are cleaned and sharpened before anything is layered on top.

68
00:07:52,733 --> 00:07:57,100
What does a modern, high-density edit actually contain?

69
00:07:57,100 --> 00:08:03,967
Here is a generic example, built for this film. It is not taken from any creator.

70
00:08:03,967 --> 00:08:08,967
At the bottom: the source footage, upscaled and colour graded.

71
00:08:08,967 --> 00:08:17,367
Above it, the subject, cut out from the background with a mask or rotoscope, so it can be separated in depth.

72
00:08:17,367 --> 00:08:26,100
Behind the subject, typography. It is huge, kinetic and partly hidden, so the subject appears to stand in front of the words.

73
00:08:26,100 --> 00:08:35,133
A 3D camera moves through these layers. Because each one sits at a different depth, they move at different speeds. That is parallax.

74
00:08:35,133 --> 00:08:41,667
Then light: glows, flares and light leaks that make separate layers feel like one image.

75
00:08:41,667 --> 00:08:46,033
Particles, grain and texture fill the empty space.

76
00:08:46,033 --> 00:08:52,900
Finally, motion blur and velocity ramps tie the whole composition to the rhythm of the track.

77
00:08:52,900 --> 00:08:58,833
Collapse the stack, and it reads as a single image moving through space.

78
00:08:58,833 --> 00:09:07,233
This is the real shift: the edit is no longer only cutting footage. It is building new images out of it.

79
00:09:07,233 --> 00:09:13,167
That takes compositing, masking, 3D, typography and timing: skills from very different disciplines.

80
00:09:17,600 --> 00:09:26,933
To make the difference concrete, give two approaches the same problem: get from shot A to shot B on a drop in the music.

81
00:09:26,933 --> 00:09:34,400
Approach one: a speed ramp into a zoom, a flash on the beat, and a shake on landing.

82
00:09:34,400 --> 00:09:40,033
It works. It is fast, it is readable, and it hits instantly.

83
00:09:40,033 --> 00:09:48,433
Approach two: a mask follows the subject's edge, the camera pushes through it, and shot B is revealed already in motion.

84
00:09:48,433 --> 00:09:57,167
Type wipes across in depth, and the curve is shaped in the graph editor so the move eases in and snaps out.

85
00:09:57,167 --> 00:10:03,700
Look at the motion curves. A linear move looks mechanical. A shaped curve has weight.

86
00:10:03,700 --> 00:10:11,200
Neither approach is wrong. But the second needs far more planning, and it is much harder to copy.

87
00:10:11,200 --> 00:10:18,667
These are generic demonstrations. We are not claiming that any of the six channels uses these exact techniques.

88
00:10:23,100 --> 00:10:27,767
So, do the numbers show the new generation winning?

89
00:10:27,767 --> 00:10:33,700
The honest answer is that the public numbers are messier than they look.

90
00:10:33,700 --> 00:10:41,500
For Slicx7, vidIQ showed about 1.52 million subscribers in February 2025. Another tracker lists 4.59 million, with no date.

91
00:10:41,500 --> 00:10:51,000
For BroxEditZ, vidIQ showed 2.78 million in April 2025. A Social Blade listing showed about 3.7 million, along with a notice that it could no longer collect data.

92
00:10:51,000 --> 00:11:00,500
For Heisty, the only YouTube figure we found is an estimate claiming more than a million. The TikTok and Snapchat profiles show entirely different audiences.

93
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:08,900
These figures come from different dates, different trackers and even different platforms, and some may not refer to the same channel.

94
00:11:08,900 --> 00:11:15,467
So this film will not rank these creators. The evidence does not support a ranking.

95
00:11:15,467 --> 00:11:23,567
What we can say is narrower: by vidIQ's figures, BroxEditZ passed two million subscribers less than three years after joining.

96
00:11:23,567 --> 00:11:30,400
And none of these numbers can tell us why. Subscriber counts do not measure editing complexity.

97
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:39,433
To show that complex editing causes growth, you would need to compare many channels, upload by upload, controlling for subject, timing and frequency.

98
00:11:39,433 --> 00:11:45,067
No one has published that study. For now, it remains a hypothesis.

99
00:11:49,500 --> 00:11:54,167
If complexity alone cannot explain the shift, what can?

100
00:11:54,167 --> 00:12:01,033
Here are five explanations. Each one is a hypothesis with supporting evidence, not a proven fact.

101
00:12:01,033 --> 00:12:09,767
One: the stage changed. Shorts became a paid, swipe-ranked feed, and newer editors built their style for that feed from the start.

102
00:12:09,767 --> 00:12:17,533
Two: subject gravity. Football and blockbuster film already have vast audiences, and edits about them can ride that attention.

103
00:12:17,533 --> 00:12:21,733
Three: the name becomes the style.

104
00:12:21,733 --> 00:12:29,833
Search YouTube and you will find tutorials titled 'Heisty like', 'Eagle Stan like', '10Mov like', 'Fictic like' and 'Slicx7 like'.

105
00:12:29,833 --> 00:12:36,100
When other editors teach your look by name, your look has become a brand.

106
00:12:36,100 --> 00:12:43,567
Four: diffusion. Many of those tutorials recreate After Effects styles in mobile apps like Alight Motion and CapCut.

107
00:12:43,567 --> 00:12:51,967
Once a style can be copied on a phone, it stops being rare, and its originators are pushed to go further.

108
00:12:51,967 --> 00:12:57,900
That is an arms race. Every signature move has a shorter shelf life.

109
00:12:57,900 --> 00:13:05,400
Five: leverage. Scripts, presets and AI tools let one editor do work that once needed far more time.

110
00:13:05,400 --> 00:13:09,767
Put together, the answer starts to look different.

111
00:13:09,767 --> 00:13:19,267
The new generation did not only edit better. They edited for a different stage, about subjects with huge audiences, in styles that became recognisable brands.

112
00:13:19,267 --> 00:13:25,200
Complexity is part of the story. It may not be the whole story.

113
00:13:29,633 --> 00:13:34,633
This creates a strange paradox about the barrier to entry.

114
00:13:34,633 --> 00:13:42,400
The floor has never been lower. A phone app and a free preset can produce a convincing velocity edit.

115
00:13:42,400 --> 00:13:51,133
But the ceiling has never been higher. A top-tier composition can demand After Effects, plugins, an AI upscaler and a powerful computer.

116
00:13:51,133 --> 00:13:57,667
So it is easier than ever to start, and harder than ever to stand out.

117
00:13:57,667 --> 00:14:03,000
For established editors, this is not a story about losing talent.

118
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:09,233
The grammar they built is now everywhere. That is exactly what made it ordinary.

119
00:14:09,233 --> 00:14:17,033
Some audiences moved to new formats. Some editors moved with them. Others kept a style they still believe in.

120
00:14:17,033 --> 00:14:24,500
A drop in engagement can reflect format changes, recommendation shifts, upload pauses or audience fatigue, not only quality.

121
00:14:24,500 --> 00:14:30,433
Without each channel's own analytics, no outsider can say which of those applies.

122
00:14:34,867 --> 00:14:39,067
So where does editing go from here?

123
00:14:39,067 --> 00:14:45,600
In June 2025, YouTube said it would bring Google's Veo 3 video-generation model into Shorts.

124
00:14:45,600 --> 00:14:49,800
From this point on, everything is speculation.

125
00:14:49,800 --> 00:14:56,667
If generated footage becomes cheap, visual density may stop being rare, just as velocity edits did.

126
00:14:56,667 --> 00:15:03,833
The scarce resource could shift again, from effects to taste: the choice of moment, music and meaning.

127
00:15:03,833 --> 00:15:12,233
The next generation might be defined not by how many layers they stack, but by what they choose to leave out.

128
00:15:13,233 --> 00:15:19,167
So, did editing evolve so far that the old standard stopped being enough?

129
00:15:19,167 --> 00:15:24,800
Partly, yes. The craft genuinely expanded, from manipulating time to constructing images.

130
00:15:24,800 --> 00:15:31,033
But the stage changed too: vertical feeds, paid Shorts, swipe metrics and global fandoms.

131
00:15:31,033 --> 00:15:36,967
The new generation mastered the craft and the stage at the same time.

132
00:15:36,967 --> 00:15:41,667
The older generation built the language they are speaking.

133
00:15:41,667 --> 00:15:45,867
Neither story makes sense without the other.

134
00:15:45,867 --> 00:15:50,233
The standard did not just rise. It moved.

135
00:15:50,233 --> 00:15:54,433
This has been VINIXEDZ.
